'''Michelangelo Valentino''' (19 March 1793 – 15 March 1875) was a [[Balisca]]n statesman, reformist and military leader who rose to prominence during the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], and later fought in several destructive campaigns against the [[Xunta Nacionalista]] during the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. He served as the first [[President of the Baliscan Confederation|President of Balisca]] from 1838 until his death in 1875, making him the longest-serving head of state in Baliscan history. His tenure oversaw the transition of the Confederation from a fractious war-torn alliance into a centralized federal state, though his increasingly authoritarian methods and imperial pretensions ultimately provoked the [[Cantonal Revolution]] of 1873–1880.

Valentino led the successful effort to enforce the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]] against resurgent Bayaran nationalists, earning him the honorary title "[[W:Caudillo|Defender of the Confederation]]" from the [[Confederal Congress]] in the immediate post-war period. During the early years of his presidency, he supported the transition of Balisca to a federal republic and oversaw the lengthy [[Anyos Federales|reconstruction process]] following the devastation of the Civil War. He championed wide-reaching universal rights including [[W:Free speech|free speech]], [[W:Freedom of worship|freedom of worship]], and [[W:Convivencia|religious harmony]], and encouraged acceptance of [[W:Sexual minority|sexual minorities]] in a departure from the sectarianism that had characterized the colonial and early republican periods.

However, Valentino's governance grew increasingly centralized and autocratic over his nearly four decades in office. He oversaw the development of the [[District of Castejón|confederal capital in Castejón]], which at the time was overwhelmed with refugees displaced during the fighting of the civil war, and laid the groundwork for the expansion of Castejón into a [[W:World city|world city]] on what he termed a "complete and humane" scale. His administration pursued systematic centralization across economic, political, and military domains, concentrating resources in the capital region while extracting wealth from the outer provinces. He developed an extensive network of patronage through the distribution of [[W:Spoils system|government positions and contracts]], creating a [[W:Oligarchy|class of dependent elites]] whose fortunes were tied to his administration.

Valentino's imperial attitude, lavish spending habits, and authoritarian tendencies increasingly alienated both the general population and elements within the political establishment. The construction of a presidential palace, the [[Plaza de Hesperides]], a grand opera house, and expansive boulevards in Castejón—intended to demonstrate Balisca's industrial progress—instead became symbols of corruption and misplaced priorities in a country where many communities remained impoverished. His retention of the confederal army as a permanent federal force of 250,000 men, rather than demobilizing after the Civil War, provided him with the means to enforce federal authority but also represented a constant reminder of centralized power in regions that had previously maintained their own military forces.

