== Later years and death ==
{{Further|Cantonal Revolution}}
By the early 1870s, opposition to Valentino's centralization had coalesced into the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' movement, which drew support from [[Trade unions in Balisca|labor unions]], [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous communities]], progressive intellectuals, and regional political movements opposed to the concentration of power in the capital. On 15 March 1875, following a meeting with his cabinet regarding the escalating crisis, Valentino died suddenly of a heart attack at the age of 82. His death created a succession crisis, as he had not designated a clear heir and had systematically eliminated potential rivals within his administration. After several days of behind-the-scenes negotiations among military leaders, cabinet ministers, and representatives of the aristocracy, [[Josèp Bernat]], the [[Secretariat of Defense (Balisca)#History|Minister of War]] and a close ally of Valentino, was appointed as the new president.

Valentino's death did not end the crisis that many of his administration's policies had provoked. In June 1873, the [[Confederalist Party]] and [[National Democratic Party]] proposed [[Proposed 1873 Baliscan constitutional amendments|constitutional amendments]] extending presidential terms from four to six years, eliminating term limits, and granting the president power to dissolve provincial legislatures. When the [[Confederal Congress]] initially rejected these proposals, Valentino ordered the legislature and called for new elections under provisional rules favoring his supporters—moves widely denounced as a [[W:Constitutional coup|constitutional coup]]. 

On 11 August 1873, workers in [[Macaio]] seized the [[Cabildo of Macaio|Cabildo]] and declared the establishment of an [[Canton of Macaio|autonomous canton]], triggering the Cantonal Revolution.

== Legacy ==
Valentino's legacy remains deeply contested in Baliscan history. His supporters emphasize his role in preserving the Confederation during the Civil War, his championing of [[W:Universal rights|universal rights]] in the early post-war period, and his vision of Castejón as a modern capital. His critics, however, point to the destruction of federal traditions, the extraction of resources from the provinces to benefit the capital, the patronage networks that corrupted Baliscan politics, and the centralization that provoked a catastrophic civil conflict. The Cantonal Revolution that followed his death represented, in many ways, the fulfillment of warnings he had ignored about the dangers of concentrating power. His presidency stands as a cautionary tale about the transition from wartime emergency powers to permanent authoritarianism, and the 1883 Confederal Convention that drafted the shadow constitution saw itself explicitly as repudiating the centralized state Valentino had constructed.

